WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.440
It's great to welcome back to the program
today.

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.090
Richard Wolff, who is of course professor
Ameritus

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.990
economics at my undergraduate Alma Mater,
the University of Massachusetts, currently

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:14.440
a visiting professor at the new school university
in New York City.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:20.619
The latest book called Understanding Marxism
is available all of the normal places including

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:23.130
in digital form a professor wolf.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.130
Great to have you on.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.130
Again.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:26.130
Glad to be here.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.130
It really, yeah.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:32.290
So let's start digging into some of the ideas
that we are seeing put forward by the 2020

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.400
democratic challengers.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:40.070
On the one hand, we've sort of seen the mainstreaming
of some ideas that even as recently as four

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:47.640
years ago were considered relatively niche
and left including um, oh, uh, a wealth tax

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:53.070
including medicare for all in a sort of more
serious and particular way.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.840
What's your overall sense of this shift?

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:01.280
And do you think that there is any policy
in particular that goes far enough in terms

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.390
of the type of systemic change that may be
needed?

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:10.290
Or are we really still talking about sort
of bandaids and fixing things around the edges?

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:16.759
Well, I basically think that to get to the
core of your question that we are still seeing

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:23.109
the vast preponderance of proposals from the
Democratic contenders.

00:01:23.109 --> 00:01:25.229
Those are bandaids.

00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:26.229
That's right.

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:28.970
Those are the attempt, uh, to see.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:35.270
Honestly, I'm certainly better than the Republicans
can manage what the failures and flaws and

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:39.590
problems of our economic and political system
are.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:46.240
Uh, but I do think it's still early in the
process of facing up to all of it so that

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.119
they are emotionally, uh, bandaids.

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:56.490
They are mostly attempts to take what we already
have some of and make it more comprehensive,

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:04.270
make it more generous and hope against hope
really, that these adjustments will somehow

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:05.920
be adequate.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.950
What I do find remarkable and I see it in
the shifting over to Medicare for all and

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.440
taxing the wealth.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:20.980
There is the beginning of an understanding
that these bandaids have to go than one used

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:28.360
to think bandaids had to go that it wasn't
just a little tweaking of the tax rates and

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:35.530
so forth, but something as fundamental as
a imposing a tax on wealth itself in the form

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:41.100
of stocks and bonds, which this country has
avoided doing a for most of its history.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:49.590
So it is a recognition that we need more and
yet our hope that relatively modest things

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:54.620
will be sufficient to what to change what's
going on.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:03:01.290
I think that's the real cool, really interesting
thing that capitalism and America is now produced

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:10.710
such an extreme level of inequality, such
an extreme experience of capitalism's instability.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:19.050
The crash of 2008 that 10 years we've just
lost trying quote unquote to recover from

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.050
it.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:25.560
The fact that the entire financial community
knows that either this year or next year we're

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.110
going to have a recession and who knows how
that this one will be.

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:39.020
All of this royaling of the realities we live
in is pushing our politics on the right over

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.740
toward Mr. Trump and all that he represents.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:46.550
But on the left in the directions that your
question that raises,

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:52.000
I saw a critique of a that you, you were involved
in in some way, shape or form.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.270
I forget exactly on what show related to the
idea of universal basic income, sort of in

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:59.800
the mode that Andrew Yang has been proposing
it.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.850
And I've talked about this a lot, including
with him and I have some problems with the

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:07.240
particulars, but to sort of paraphrase, and
I hope I'm doing it correctly, one of your

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:13.320
more general criticisms was that giving people
$1,000 a month is not going to solve a lot

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:18.450
of the structural issues that exist regarding
inequality, regarding the relationship between

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.829
most workers and their employers, et Cetera,
et cetera.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:27.160
And I thought that that was a good critique,
but I wondered couldn't the same opposition

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:32.310
besides good against food stamps, against
WIC, et Cetera, et cetera.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:37.990
And it doesn't strike me as a reason not to
do it as long as we sort of recognize its

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:39.780
limitations so to speak.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:44.650
So I guess to clarify, are you against something
like universal basic income period?

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:49.360
Like are you against doing it or is it merely
that you want to make sure that we understand

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.360
that it's not structurally changing the problems
of the system?

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:54.920
It's mostly the latter.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:05:01.220
I don't oppose doing these things any more
than I oppose giving welfare to, to people

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.010
who are denied the job.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:12.030
But because the, the liberal part of American
politics has long ago given up the critique

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:18.440
of the structure, the notion of basic change,
the whole debate of the 20th century about

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.530
capitalism versus socialism, which by the
way is coming back now.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:29.910
But because the liberals gave up on it, people
like me have to hammer at the inadequacy of

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:37.530
these bandaids less the idea the that we either
have no bandaid or we have a bandaid.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:43.090
But that's something beyond the bandaid is
somehow unthinkable or undiscussable.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:50.139
So I want to point out that for example, if
you give everybody a guaranteed basic income,

00:05:50.139 --> 00:05:55.560
but some people go to work and have a job
and earn way more than all of that, you're

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:02.360
just inviting what our old history shows us,
which is sooner or later the people who work

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:06.870
for and end a living will be, how shall I
put it?

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:13.100
Politely helped to see the people who get
the guaranteed income as in some sense living

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:19.760
off the taxes they pay and therefore the tension
between the employed and the unemployed that

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:27.620
has always dogged capitalism will simply resurface
in an adjusted form around the question of

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:28.950
a basic income.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:37.360
Therefore, I would much rather face the reality,
which is if we have technological change that

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:42.860
makes it possible for fewer people to be more
productive than they used to be.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:48.290
So that instead of needing a hundred workers
to make something, we only need 50 we had,

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:52.419
we can cut the labor costs in half.

00:06:52.419 --> 00:06:55.650
Capitalism's solves that problem in a particular
way.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:07:02.139
It fires have the people, right, because then
you can produce the same amount with half

00:07:02.139 --> 00:07:03.760
the people sell it.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:09.270
Let's presume for simplicity at the same price
and what you get as revenue.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:12.110
You now don't have to pay half the workers
you use.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:14.570
Do you keep that money as your profit?

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:18.889
Which is why the technological change attracts
you in the first place.

00:07:18.889 --> 00:07:22.800
But that's the capitalist answer, but it ought
to be hours.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:23.800
Why?

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:27.760
Because the alternative way of handling a
doubling in productivity, which is what we've

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:33.970
just discussed, would be to give everybody
half the amount of hours per day to work.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:36.310
That way everybody keeps their job.

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:38.140
Everybody keeps their income.

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:44.190
What the benefit of technology is, what we
really always promised people, namely leisure

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:49.600
and in the society, not driven by profit,
that the technology would be advanced because

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:56.070
the game is to the majority in more leisure
rather than the game to a minority in the

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:57.900
way of more profit.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:02.180
These are the questions that are structural
and basic and these are the ones that still

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:08.810
remain a bit too scary for virtually all of
the candidates in the Democratic Party.

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:10.270
To to get ahold of.

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:14.590
I actually did a commentary yesterday about
the latest jobs numbers and I said it's fine

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:17.070
to talk about 75,000

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:18.440
versus the expected number.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:23.780
It's fine to talk about 0.2% wage growth versus
0.5, but fundamentally none of this stuff

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:28.880
is actually addressing these incoming and
growing issues of the relationship of humans

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:29.880
to work.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:34.810
And my analysis of the hundred employees versus
50 due to the technological change has been,

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:39.060
and I wonder if you agree with it that the
pure free market capitalist, so, uh, sort

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:44.970
of, um, uh, reception of that technological
change that allows you to double productivity

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:46.640
would be you fire half the workers.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:51.480
And that's also bad for other elements of
the economy because now you have 50 workers

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:55.720
that no longer have money with which to buy
things from other businesses.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:01.230
The pure socialist solution might be, well,
we've socialized ownership of the company

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:04.650
to begin with, so the workers share in all
the gains.

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:09.210
Now, here's where I wonder if we disagreed
the social democracy.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:17.339
Uh, a solution might be the business owners
are entitled to some piece of that productivity

00:09:17.339 --> 00:09:21.940
gain, whereas some significant portion would
be divided up by the workers.

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:22.940
Is that fair?

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:24.050
Is that pie in the sky?

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:30.910
No, I think that's a perfectly reasonable,
I mean it assume something namely that the

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:38.160
owners are in some sense different from the
people who are working there really change

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:39.210
the system.

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:42.900
That difference would disappear, but let's
keep it for a while.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:47.820
Let's assume that there are owners are capitalists
on the one hand and workers on the other.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:50.430
It would be an enormous step forward.

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:55.430
I would agree to have a discussion in which
the question is raised.

00:09:55.430 --> 00:10:01.560
Okay, we now have a technical advance we can
install that year in the business we have

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:03.710
and that gives us what?

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:05.570
It gives us a choice.

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:11.690
We can either use all of it for profit and
fire half the workers or we can use none of

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:17.870
it for profit and give all the workers basically
I have time employment situation at the same

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:21.080
wage or, and this is simple arithmetic.

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:22.470
We can split the difference.

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:29.110
We can have a conversation, a discussion,
maybe a vote as to what portion of the games

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:37.649
from a technological advance are given to
workers in the form of leisure versus employers

00:10:37.649 --> 00:10:39.720
in the form of profits.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:46.680
That already would be a level of the democratization
of the workplace, which is what I stress anyway

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:49.070
would be a major step forward.

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:56.330
And by the way, while this is a revolutionary
idea here in the United States, social democracies

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:59.220
in Europe, this is not a new idea.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:06.060
This is in fact what goes on in Germany and
France and Italy, unions often or bargaining

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:14.130
with employers on precisely how to divide
technical and other kinds of advances in terms

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:17.160
of sharing in a sense in the benefit.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:23.080
It's not revolutionary, it's not challenging
the private ownership of the industries and

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:25.290
the empathy and the enterprises.

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:30.540
But it is saying that what happens with an
advance of technology is something that has

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:36.020
to be discussed and worked out like everything
else about the workplace and not something

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:41.970
simply to be determined to the advantage of
one side and the exclusion of participation

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:42.970
on the other.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:48.250
Well, that gets me to something else I want
to hear your thoughts on which is that there's

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:55.990
a very specific and productive, I think, debate
to be had about capitalism versus socialism,

00:11:55.990 --> 00:12:01.390
social democracy versus unregulated capitalism,
et Cetera, but are you concerned about what

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:07.480
I have seen as sort of some confusion between
social democracy and socialism?

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:11.940
I've done a number of segments where I'm saying
if we even really want to seriously have this

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:16.061
debate, we have to make sure that we're calling
things what they are and socialism, where

00:12:16.061 --> 00:12:22.330
the means of production is socialized is different
than something like the Swedish system or

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:27.920
the Danish system, which is a capitalist system
that says we are going to use more of the

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:34.090
government revenue to ensure that the standard
of living doesn't go below a certain level.

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:38.800
Is that an academic distinction or is that
a serious distinction that we need to understand

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:40.720
to really talk about this stuff?

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:44.740
I could not be more 100% the latter and not
the format.

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:51.709
In other words, this is not academic or abstract
or nitpicking or anything like that.

00:12:51.709 --> 00:12:53.700
This is of the essence.

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:58.770
And that's part of our, our backwardness here
in the United States for a half a century

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:06.360
at the, after the end of World War, socialism,
communism, Marxism, all of that was prescribed.

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:08.350
It was declared trailers.

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:10.360
It was declared evil.

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:12.380
It was whatever you want.

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:17.269
And people were good Americans who kept away
from it, didn't think about it, didn't read

00:13:17.269 --> 00:13:20.209
about it, didn't debate about it, nothing.

00:13:20.209 --> 00:13:26.740
And now that capitalism has shown itself to
need basic change to an awful lot of people

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:31.840
for whom it is a working real well, there's
a process of rediscovery and, and that's all

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:33.010
to the good.

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:41.030
But in that process, you're right, the confusion
and the misuse of language and the naming

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:46.250
of things that really ought to be named that
way because a hundred years of literature

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:49.959
and a debate, uh, had clarified these differences.

00:13:49.959 --> 00:13:51.390
But Americans are just learning.

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:53.079
Let me give you an example.

00:13:53.079 --> 00:14:00.070
Two weeks ago, I was invited and I did it
to be on the town hall of the Fox television

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:07.440
system for an hour answering questions from
a live audience, uh, debating, by the way,

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:14.990
Lou Dobbs and Neil Cavuto and all those people
sitting next to me, uh, Herman Jane or the

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:19.820
candidate for president so that we had a debate
was capitalism versus socialism and they're

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:29.459
on display was this arresting fudge of words
and ideas in which the same word socialism

00:14:29.459 --> 00:14:35.800
was applied to Sweden, to Italy, to China,
to Russia, to Venezuela.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:42.260
I need the chaos and confusion that is being
sown now, partly on the other side, they want

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:44.430
the lump all of these things together, right?

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:45.600
I understand that.

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:50.820
But if you're interested in these things as
real options for our society in difficulty,

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:57.420
which I am and many Americans are, that has
to be clarified and I think is very good.

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:02.760
Even if it's difficult that you begin to say,
look, this is social democracy or this is

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:04.860
democratic socialism.

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:09.920
This is something else too, which we can give
a variety of names that have been used, but

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:16.970
we have to begin clarifying this so we can
have the conversation and the political action,

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:20.200
uh, focus on what it is we actually want.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:21.200
Well that's good.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:25.149
It makes me feel better that I haven't been
wasting my time with, uh, with those segments.

00:15:25.149 --> 00:15:27.430
We've been speaking with economist Richard
Wolf.

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:30.810
The new book is called understanding Marxism.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:32.391
It's available everywhere you went in.

00:15:32.391 --> 00:15:34.450
Imagine books are available.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:36.330
Uh, Professor Wolf, always great to talk to
you.

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:37.839
They could talk to you David and hope we can
do it again soon.

